# DURABLE REPRO — moving out of a `&`-parameter passes `gg check` (with a FALSE
# warning) and then PANICS the compiler.
#
# WRONG TODAY (measured at HEAD):
#   `gg check` -> warning: parameter `&v` is never mutated — consider removing `&`
#                 OK: no semantic errors
#   `gg build` -> PANIC:
#       Tier 2a consume-site violation: fn @steal bb0 i1 —
#       AssignIntoOwnedSlot(dst: Vector__int64_t)
#       — borrowed source consumed at consuming position
#       at src/ir/lowering/mod.rs:2127
#
# TWO defects in one program, and the warning is the more embarrassing:
#
# 1. `Vector[int] w = !v` MOVES OUT OF A BORROW. `v` is borrowed from the
#    caller, so the callee cannot give it away. That is a check-time error, and
#    the lowering validator is right to refuse it — but a validator panic is not
#    a diagnostic. The user gets a compiler crash for a type error.
#
# 2. THE WARNING IS FALSE, AND IT ADVISES THE OPPOSITE OF THE FIX. It says `&v`
#    is "never mutated — consider removing `&`", when the body CONSUMES `v`.
#    Taking that advice (dropping the `&`) does not help; the move is still
#    out of a borrowed bare param. The mutation check evidently does not count
#    `!v` as a use of the parameter.
#
# INTENDED: a check-time rejection naming the real problem — a `&`-parameter is
# borrowed and cannot be moved out of — and no warning claiming the parameter is
# unused. Never a panic, and never advice that points away from the fix.
#
# ⚠ The Tier-2a ICE class is filed elsewhere; this TRIGGER (move out of an
# `&`-param) and the false warning are not.
#
# The vector is built, not a literal, so nothing here is const-folded away.

void steal(Vector[int] &v):
    Vector[int] w = !v
    print(w.len())

void main():
    Vector[int] a = [1, 2, 3]
    a.push(4)
    steal(&a)
